<!--
  ============================================================================
  BANYNOVA — BASE LAYOUT (Blogger Theme Foundation)
  Version 2.0 — rebuilt to comply with BANYNOVA ENGINEERING STANDARDS
  (Parts 1-9 and 12: Core Principles, Blogger XML, HTML, CSS, JavaScript,
  UI Components, Accessibility WCAG 2.2 AA, Performance, SEO, Production
  Checklist).
  ============================================================================
  Governed by Documentation_Governance_v1.0.md, Design_System_v1.0.md, and
  the BANYNOVA ENGINEERING STANDARDS document.

  WHAT THIS FILE IS
  A COMPLETE, standalone Blogger theme file — not a fragment. Exactly one
  b:skin block, a correct html/head/body skeleton, one Header section and
  one Main section using Blogger's own Blog widget, and a Footer section.

  BEFORE YOU TOUCH ANYTHING IN BLOGGER
  1. Theme -> Backup/Restore -> Download theme. Save that file somewhere
     safe. This is the single most important step.
  2. Theme -> Edit HTML -> select all existing content -> delete it ->
     paste this entire file in its place.
  3. Use Preview BEFORE clicking Save — check homepage, a normal post,
     and mobile width.
  4. Only Save once preview looks correct.

  TWO DELIBERATE DEVIATIONS FROM THE STANDARDS DOCUMENT (flagged, not
  silently applied — Part 3 Section 3 asks for <!DOCTYPE html> and
  <html lang="en">):
  - No literal <!DOCTYPE html> is written here. Blogger's rendering engine
    injects the HTML5 doctype into the final output automatically; the
    template source itself is XML compiled by Blogger, not raw HTML sent
    to the browser. Adding a literal doctype before <html> risks an extra,
    non-standard node ahead of the required single root element.
  - <html lang='en'> IS included below (see the html tag) — that part of
    the standard is followed directly, since it carries no such conflict.

  WHAT STILL NEEDS TO BE FILLED IN
  - Replace the favicon href with your real uploaded favicon URL.
  - Replace the og:image / twitter:image placeholder URL with a real
    1200x630 social image (Part 9 Section 10).
  - Replace the placeholder social/GitHub links in the footer.
  - The post template ("post" includable) stays intentionally minimal —
    the Universal Tool Template (Roadmap Step 4) extends it once tool
    pages are being built.
  - FAQPage / BreadcrumbList JSON-LD are intentionally NOT added here —
    Part 9 Section 13 scopes those to tool pages, and structured data
    should never describe content that isn't visibly on the page yet.

  CHANGELOG (v1.0 -> v2.0)
  - New head order matches Part 2 Section 7 exactly (charset, viewport,
    theme-color, color-scheme, canonical, title, description, OG,
    Twitter, icons, structured data, CSS, critical JS).
  - Added color-scheme meta, referrer-policy meta, og:image, og:locale,
    twitter:image/title/description, and Organization / WebSite+
    SearchAction / WebPage JSON-LD (Part 9 Sections 8, 9, 12).
  - Skip link now targets id="main-content" (Part 7 Section 8).
  - New design tokens: header-height, sidebar-width, and a z-index scale
    (dropdown/sticky/modal/tooltip) replacing ad hoc z-index numbers
    (Part 4 Section 4).
  - body transition now animates background-color, not background
    (Part 4 Section 23).
  - Added img { height: auto } and a documented img/picture/svg block
    (Part 4 Section 18).
  - Added ::selection styling (Part 4 Section 32).
  - Logo SVG de-duplicated into one <symbol>, referenced via <use> in
    the header and footer (Part 2 Section 16).
  - Navigation restructured to Tools / Categories / Blog / Resources /
    About per Part 6 Section 5, with aria-current="page" wired in JS.
  - Footer expanded: Quick Links, Legal (Privacy, Terms, Cookies,
    Accessibility), Contact, plus Sitemap/RSS/GitHub — Disclaimer moved
    out of the primary Legal list into the secondary bottom bar
    (Part 6 Section 20).
  - Header scroll handler now wrapped in Banynova.utils.throttle (100ms)
    instead of an unthrottled listener (Part 5 Section 12).
  - JS reorganized under a proper Banynova.navigation module (scroll,
    mobile menu, active-link) matching the Module Architecture in
    Part 5 Section 4, still frozen per-module (not at the top level) so
    future modules — search, clipboard, etc. — can still be added.
  - Added Banynova.perf.applyLazyLoadingToPostImages(), since Blogger
    post-body <img> tags come from user content and can't be templated
    directly — this is the practical way to apply Part 2 Section 15's
    loading="lazy" / decoding="async" rule to that content, skipping the
    first image so a genuine hero/LCP image is never lazy-loaded.
  - Fixed two non-mobile-first breakpoints (Part 4 Section 11 requires
    mobile-first): h1 size, .bn-section padding, and .bn-footer-grid
    column count now define their small/mobile value as the base rule,
    scaling up via min-width media queries — previously the base rules
    held desktop values overridden down via max-width, which is the
    opposite of mobile-first.
  - v2.2 reconciliation pass: fixed a newly-found h1/h2/h3/h4 mobile
    sizing collision (h1 and h2 were both fs-3xl on mobile before the
    min-width:640 bump restored the hierarchy — now each heading level
    has its own distinct mobile-first base size). og:url now mirrors the
    canonical link's own homepage-vs-page logic instead of always using
    canonicalUrl. Added og:image:width/height, .u-hidden utility, and
    noopener/target='_blank' on the external GitHub footer link. Added
    an early, self-contained lazy-loading script positioned immediately
    after each post's body (see the "post" includable) — genuinely
    earlier than the previous end-of-document-only approach, though
    browser preload scanners mean no script-based fix can be fully
    complete; the honest limitation is documented inline. Rejected an
    unverified `<data:post.body snippet='false' loading='lazy'/>`
    approach from an earlier draft — that is not documented, working
    Blogger template syntax and would silently do nothing.
  - v2.3: merged the Hero section (homepage-only) into the homepage
    branch of the Main includable — real search form posting to
    Blogger's native /search endpoint, combobox-pattern suggestions,
    keyboard shortcut ("/"), noscript fallback for crawlers/no-JS, and
    a Banynova.register('search', {...}) module. See Section "HERO
    SECTION — INTEGRATION MAP" below for exactly where its HTML, CSS,
    and JS each live and why. Homepage sections beyond Hero (Popular
    Tools, Categories, Tutorials, Why Choose, Stats, FAQ, Newsletter)
    still inject below it as they're built.
  ============================================================================

  HERO SECTION — INTEGRATION MAP (read this before touching the Hero)
  The Hero is homepage-only content, so unlike Header/Footer it does NOT
  get its own top-level <b:section>/<b:widget> — unlike Header/Footer it
  is not sitewide chrome that Blogger recognizes as a distinct region.
  It lives entirely inside the Main widget's existing "main" includable,
  gated by the same homepage b:if that already existed there. This
  keeps exactly one Header widget, one Main/Blog widget, one Footer
  section — adding a Hero never means adding a fourth widget.

  - HTML: inside <b:includable id='main'>, in the
    `<b:if cond='data:blog.url == data:blog.homepageUrl'>` branch —
    search for "HERO (Homepage only)" below. Nothing homepage-specific
    ever belongs in the <b:else/> branch (that's the post/page renderer)
    or outside this includable entirely.
  - CSS: inside <b:skin>, Section 5 (COMPONENTS), in the Hero
    (Homepage only) block, positioned right after Main and before
    Footer — matching the same top-to-bottom order the HTML itself
    renders in.
  - JS: inside the closing <script> before </body>, as a
    Banynova.register('search', {...}) module — positioned after the
    `navigation` module and before the final freeze/init block. Its
    own init() bails out immediately if #bn-hero-search-form isn't in
    the DOM (true on every page except the homepage), so it's always
    safe to call unconditionally from the init sequence at the bottom,
    exactly like every other module.

  Any future homepage-only section (Popular Tools, Categories grid,
  etc.) should follow this exact same three-part pattern: HTML inside
  the same b:if branch, CSS in Section 5 positioned where it visually
  renders, JS as its own Banynova.register()'d module called
  unconditionally at the bottom.
  ============================================================================
-->
<html b:css='false' b:defaultwidgetversion='2' b:layoutsVersion='3' b:responsive='true' expr:dir='data:blog.languageDirection' lang='en' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml' xmlns:b='http://www.google.com/2005/gml/b' xmlns:data='http://www.google.com/2005/gml/data' xmlns:expr='http://www.google.com/2005/gml/expr'>
<head>
<!-- ======================= 1. CHARSET / VIEWPORT ======================= -->
<meta charset='UTF-8'/>
<meta content='width=device-width, initial-scale=1' name='viewport'/>

<!-- ======================= 2. THEME COLOR / COLOR SCHEME ======================= -->
<meta content='#2a6df4' name='theme-color'/>
<meta content='light dark' name='color-scheme'/>

<!-- ======================= 3. CANONICAL ======================= -->
<b:if cond='data:blog.url == data:blog.homepageUrl'>
<link expr:href='data:blog.homepageUrl' rel='canonical'/>
<b:else/>
<link expr:href='data:blog.canonicalUrl' rel='canonical'/>
</b:if>

<!-- ======================= 4. TITLE ======================= -->
<title><data:blog.pageTitle/></title>

<!-- ======================= 5. META DESCRIPTION / ROBOTS / AUTHOR ======================= -->
<!-- Fallback text is the approved sitewide description from the Brand Voice
     Guide — used whenever a page has no custom description set (label
     pages, some posts), so every page still gets a real snippet instead of
     Google auto-generating one from arbitrary page text. -->
<b:if cond='data:blog.metaDescription'>
<meta expr:content='data:blog.metaDescription' name='description'/>
<b:else/>
<meta content='Banynova offers free online tools for developers, marketers, creators, and students. Fast, private, browser-based utilities. No sign-up. No uploads.' name='description'/>
</b:if>
<b:if cond='data:blog.searchLabel OR data:blog.searchQuery'>
<meta content='noindex, follow' name='robots'/>
<b:else/>
<meta content='index, follow' name='robots'/>
</b:if>
<meta content='Banynova' name='author'/>
<meta content='strict-origin-when-cross-origin' name='referrer'/>

<!-- ======================= 6. OPEN GRAPH ======================= -->
<meta content='website' property='og:type'/>
<meta expr:content='data:blog.title' property='og:site_name'/>
<meta expr:content='data:blog.pageName' property='og:title'/>
<!-- og:url mirrors the canonical link's own homepage-vs-page logic (Section
     3) exactly, rather than always using canonicalUrl, so shares from the
     homepage never carry Blogger's mobile ?m=1 or other query artifacts. -->
<b:if cond='data:blog.url == data:blog.homepageUrl'>
<meta expr:content='data:blog.homepageUrl' property='og:url'/>
<b:else/>
<meta expr:content='data:blog.canonicalUrl' property='og:url'/>
</b:if>
<b:if cond='data:blog.metaDescription'>
<meta expr:content='data:blog.metaDescription' property='og:description'/>
<b:else/>
<meta content='Banynova offers free online tools for developers, marketers, creators, and students. Fast, private, browser-based utilities. No sign-up. No uploads.' property='og:description'/>
</b:if>
<meta content='https://banynova.blogspot.com/og-image.png' property='og:image'/>
<meta content='1200' property='og:image:width'/>
<meta content='630' property='og:image:height'/>
<meta content='en_US' property='og:locale'/>

<!-- ======================= 7. TWITTER CARDS ======================= -->
<meta content='summary_large_image' name='twitter:card'/>
<meta expr:content='data:blog.pageName' name='twitter:title'/>
<b:if cond='data:blog.metaDescription'>
<meta expr:content='data:blog.metaDescription' name='twitter:description'/>
<b:else/>
<meta content='Banynova offers free online tools for developers, marketers, creators, and students. Fast, private, browser-based utilities. No sign-up. No uploads.' name='twitter:description'/>
</b:if>
<meta content='https://banynova.blogspot.com/og-image.png' name='twitter:image'/>

<!-- ======================= 8. ICONS (replace with your uploaded favicon URL) ======================= -->
<link href='https://banynova.blogspot.com/favicon.ico' rel='icon' type='image/x-icon'/>

<!-- ======================= 9. STRUCTURED DATA (JSON-LD) ======================= -->
<script type='application/ld+json'>
{
  &quot;@context&quot;: &quot;https://schema.org&quot;,
  &quot;@type&quot;: &quot;Organization&quot;,
  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Banynova&quot;,
  &quot;url&quot;: &quot;<data:blog.homepageUrl/>&quot;,
  &quot;slogan&quot;: &quot;Powering Your Digital Workflow.&quot;
}
</script>
<script type='application/ld+json'>
{
  &quot;@context&quot;: &quot;https://schema.org&quot;,
  &quot;@type&quot;: &quot;WebSite&quot;,
  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;<data:blog.title/>&quot;,
  &quot;url&quot;: &quot;<data:blog.homepageUrl/>&quot;,
  &quot;potentialAction&quot;: {
    &quot;@type&quot;: &quot;SearchAction&quot;,
    &quot;target&quot;: &quot;<data:blog.homepageUrl/>search?q={search_term_string}&quot;,
    &quot;query-input&quot;: &quot;required name=search_term_string&quot;
  }
}
</script>
<script type='application/ld+json'>
{
  &quot;@context&quot;: &quot;https://schema.org&quot;,
  &quot;@type&quot;: &quot;WebPage&quot;,
  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;<data:blog.pageTitle/>&quot;,
  &quot;url&quot;: &quot;<data:blog.canonicalUrl/>&quot;,
  &quot;isPartOf&quot;: { &quot;@type&quot;: &quot;WebSite&quot;, &quot;url&quot;: &quot;<data:blog.homepageUrl/>&quot; }
}
</script>

<!-- ======================= 10. NO-FLASH THEME INIT (critical, runs before CSS paints) ======================= -->
<!-- Applies saved preference (LocalStorage) or system preference to <html> before first paint, per Part 2 Section 17 (prevent FOUC). -->
<script>//<![CDATA[
(function () {
  try {
    var saved = window.localStorage.getItem('bn-theme');
    var prefersDark = window.matchMedia && window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches;
    var mode = saved || (prefersDark ? 'dark' : 'light');
    document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', mode);
  } catch (e) {
    document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', 'light');
  }
})();
//]]></script>

<!-- Defensive critical CSS, adjacent to the script above (Part 8 Section 11
     treats this as legitimate above-the-fold critical CSS, distinct from
     the main design-system skin below). In this template the full skin is
     inline CDATA right after this, so the practical FOUC window is already
     near-zero — this is belt-and-suspenders: it guarantees the background
     flips the instant data-theme is set, with zero dependency on how much
     of the skin the browser has parsed yet. Kept intentionally to just the
     two background colors, not a duplicate of the token system. -->
<style>
html[data-theme="dark"] { background-color: hsl(222, 47%, 11%); }
html[data-theme="light"] { background-color: hsl(220, 20%, 98%); }
</style>

<b:include data='blog' name='all-head-content'/>

<!-- ======================= 11. THE ONE AND ONLY SKIN (CSS) ======================= -->
<b:skin><![CDATA[

/* ==========================================================================
   1. DESIGN TOKENS — see Design_System_v1.0.md Sections 2-8 (source of truth)
   ========================================================================== */
:root {
